# DTF tier manifest: oidc-group-preprovision  (artifact-keeper#4296, closes #4297)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + sso=oidc (the headless mock IdP from
# profiles/sso.oidc.yml, script in tiers/oidc-group-sync/fixtures/mock_oidc.py).
#
# THE FEATURE (#4297, shipped by #4296, milestone 1.12.0)
# Before #4296 an OIDC-managed group (groups.external_source='oidc', owned by
# one provider) only came into existence on the first login whose claims named
# it, so an operator could not grant it repository permissions ahead of time.
# #4296 adds an admin-only endpoint:
#   POST /api/v1/admin/sso/oidc/{provider_id}/groups  {"name": "<group>"}
# It reuses the login path's ownership-safe upsert (ensure_federated_group):
#   * 200 + {id, name, external_source:"oidc", external_provider_id} and no
#     membership; the same call again returns the SAME id (idempotent, 200);
#   * 409 when the name is owned by a local group or another provider;
#   * 404 for an unknown provider, 400 when the provider's
#     map_groups_to_groups is off or the name is empty;
#   * behind admin_middleware (non-admin refused) and require_admin().
# Membership stays IdP-managed: the groups API refuses manual member edits on
# a federated group (409, #2874), and a later OIDC login whose claim names the
# group joins THE SAME row, while a login without it prunes the membership.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      admin pre-provision creates the row (oidc, provider, 0 members);
#              idempotent repeat; manual membership writes on it refused; a
#              later login joins the same id with no duplicate row and a login
#              without the claim prunes it; the ownership / provider / name
#              guards (409/409/404/400/400). RED on 1.11.3: the route is 404
#              and the group only appears after a first login.
#   (CONTROL)  must hold on BOTH images: the mock IdP serves discovery + JWKS;
#              a non-admin POST to the route is refused (403 on the fix, 404
#              on 1.11.3) and creates no row; login-time group sync
#              auto-creates an oidc group, attaches the user, refuses a manual
#              member write (409), and prunes the membership when the claim
#              disappears.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: a dozen admin calls and SSO logins back to back
# (same as oidc-group-sync).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem sso.oidc"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
